1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,118
Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:01,137 --> 00:00:03,683
Or, or good afternoon, or good
evening, wherever you are.

3
00:00:03,775 --> 00:00:06,767
my name is Mohan Kumar Dalai,
and I will be talking about,

4
00:00:06,847 --> 00:00:11,421
AI-driven maintenance, transforming
reliability in modern power plants.

5
00:00:12,849 --> 00:00:16,591
When we think about reliability in
power generation, we often think

6
00:00:16,593 --> 00:00:22,465
about availability, forced outages,
maintenance costs, and asset performance.

7
00:00:23,369 --> 00:00:24,994
But today's challenge is different.

8
00:00:25,961 --> 00:00:30,829
Power plants generate enormous amount
of operational data every second.

9
00:00:32,055 --> 00:00:36,409
The question is no longer where we
have the data or where we have data.

10
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:43,389
The question is whether we can transform
that data into a actionable maintenance

11
00:00:43,419 --> 00:00:45,715
intelligence before a failure occurs.

12
00:00:46,776 --> 00:00:52,623
Today, I would like to discuss how
artificial intelligence is helping

13
00:00:52,624 --> 00:00:59,745
maintenance organizations move from
reactive to failures toward anticipating

14
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:06,396
them, while still preserving engineering
judgment and operational accountability.

15
00:01:07,346 --> 00:01:12,603
By end of this session, I hope you
all see AI not as a replacement

16
00:01:12,606 --> 00:01:18,058
for maintenance expertise, but
as a force multiplier for it.

17
00:01:19,261 --> 00:01:23,458
Let's begin with the core reliability
problem that every plant faces.

18
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:29,760
Before we discuss AI, we first
need to understand why traditional

19
00:01:29,791 --> 00:01:34,755
maintenance approaches struggle
with increasingly complex assets.

20
00:01:36,156 --> 00:01:39,074
Historically, we have
relied on two approaches.

21
00:01:39,725 --> 00:01:42,000
The first is reactive maintenance.

22
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:45,155
We fix equipment after it fails.

23
00:01:46,079 --> 00:01:48,616
The second is time-based maintenance.

24
00:01:48,732 --> 00:01:52,920
We perform inspections or
replacements on a fixed schedule.

25
00:01:54,104 --> 00:01:57,981
Both approaches have value,
but, both have limitations too.

26
00:01:59,302 --> 00:02:03,275
A turbine bearing does not
suddenly fail overnight.

27
00:02:04,172 --> 00:02:07,498
A transformer does not
instantly develop a fault.

28
00:02:08,754 --> 00:02:14,797
Most failures evolve gradually and
leave warning signs along the way.

29
00:02:16,052 --> 00:02:20,148
That's where condition-based
thinking becomes important.

30
00:02:21,319 --> 00:02:27,254
Rather than asking when maintenance
was last performed, we ask, "What is

31
00:02:27,254 --> 00:02:29,136
the equipment telling us right now?"

32
00:02:30,285 --> 00:02:34,355
That shift in mindset creates
the foundation for everything

33
00:02:34,362 --> 00:02:39,555
we are discussing today Once we
begin listening to an equipment

34
00:02:39,563 --> 00:02:42,146
condition, a new challenge appears.

35
00:02:43,145 --> 00:02:46,917
Not all data tells the same story, right?

36
00:02:46,917 --> 00:02:48,485
So the context is matter.

37
00:02:49,576 --> 00:02:54,631
Many organizations assume more data
automatically leads to better decisions.

38
00:02:55,667 --> 00:02:57,929
Unfortunately, that's not always true.

39
00:02:59,495 --> 00:03:03,289
Consider a vibration
increase on a rotating asset.

40
00:03:04,494 --> 00:03:05,522
Is it degradation?

41
00:03:07,169 --> 00:03:08,974
Was there a recent startup?

42
00:03:10,555 --> 00:03:12,312
Did load conditions change?

43
00:03:13,833 --> 00:03:17,268
Was ambient temperature unusually high?

44
00:03:17,811 --> 00:03:20,188
Or is the sensor itself drifting?

45
00:03:21,356 --> 00:03:24,550
Without context, all these
possibilities can look similar.

46
00:03:25,895 --> 00:03:32,260
AI becomes valuable because it evaluates
relationships between operating

47
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:37,088
conditions rather than evaluating
individual data points in isolation.

48
00:03:38,561 --> 00:03:42,828
This allows us to distinguish
among normal behavior, measurement

49
00:03:42,830 --> 00:03:45,254
error, and genuine degradation.

50
00:03:46,609 --> 00:03:50,271
The real goal isn't collecting more
information, it's understanding

51
00:03:50,271 --> 00:03:52,453
what information actually means.

52
00:03:54,109 --> 00:03:58,117
And that raises an important
question: What types of AI help

53
00:03:58,213 --> 00:04:00,131
us achieve that understanding?

54
00:04:01,131 --> 00:04:04,809
Many people hear the term AI and
think of a single technology.

55
00:04:06,107 --> 00:04:08,595
In reality, multiple
technologies work together.

56
00:04:10,388 --> 00:04:14,802
Machine learning helps us
recognize operational patterns.

57
00:04:15,807 --> 00:04:20,787
Deep learning excels at extracting
information from complex signals such

58
00:04:20,787 --> 00:04:24,161
as vibration, acoustics, and imagery.

59
00:04:25,262 --> 00:04:29,131
Expert systems preserve decades
of engineering knowledge through

60
00:04:29,134 --> 00:04:31,115
rules and diagnostic logic.

61
00:04:32,408 --> 00:04:37,595
Natural language processing allows us to
learn from work orders, technician notes,

62
00:04:37,785 --> 00:04:40,192
inspection reports, and operating logs.

63
00:04:41,505 --> 00:04:45,888
The strongest maintenance systems
don't rely on one approach.

64
00:04:46,426 --> 00:04:52,261
They combine statistical detection,
engineering expertise, historical

65
00:04:52,261 --> 00:04:54,775
experience, and operational knowledge.

66
00:04:56,027 --> 00:04:58,883
The objective is not
artificial intelligence.

67
00:04:59,919 --> 00:05:02,553
The objective is better
maintenance decisions.

68
00:05:04,037 --> 00:05:09,128
Let's look at how that works in part--
in practical power plant applications.

69
00:05:09,971 --> 00:05:11,742
Let's start with the rotating machinery.

70
00:05:13,128 --> 00:05:17,574
Rotating equipment remains one
of the most critical reliability

71
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:19,207
concerns in power generation.

72
00:05:20,625 --> 00:05:25,214
Whether we are discussing
turbines, generators, pumps,

73
00:05:25,677 --> 00:05:29,878
or bearings, degradation
usually develops progressively.

74
00:05:31,143 --> 00:05:36,682
Traditionally, analysts review vibration
data and look for familiar signatures.

75
00:05:38,393 --> 00:05:43,053
AI expands that capability by
continuously evaluating thousands of

76
00:05:43,053 --> 00:05:45,564
operating conditions simultaneously.

77
00:05:47,393 --> 00:05:52,264
Instead of relying on fixed
thresholds, the system l-learns what

78
00:05:52,302 --> 00:05:54,663
normal looks like for each machine.

79
00:05:56,567 --> 00:06:01,026
That distinction is important because
equipment operating at different loads may

80
00:06:01,026 --> 00:06:03,291
naturally produce different signatures.

81
00:06:03,678 --> 00:06:07,706
The result is earlier identification
of lubrication problems,

82
00:06:07,771 --> 00:06:10,629
misalignment, imbalance, and wear.

83
00:06:11,548 --> 00:06:14,721
Most importantly, alert quality improves.

84
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:19,935
Fewer false alarms means
greater trust The same principle

85
00:06:19,935 --> 00:06:21,814
applies beyond rotating assets.

86
00:06:22,117 --> 00:06:26,537
Let's move to process systems,
like your boilers or heat systems.

87
00:06:27,807 --> 00:06:31,078
Process equipment present
a very different challenge.

88
00:06:32,454 --> 00:06:37,209
A boiler cannot be understood
from a single instrument reading.

89
00:06:38,809 --> 00:06:44,042
Combustion efficiency, fuel quality,
airflow, temperatures, emissions, and

90
00:06:44,042 --> 00:06:46,493
control actions interact continuously.

91
00:06:47,846 --> 00:06:52,452
AI helps identify deviations
from expected relationships.

92
00:06:53,569 --> 00:06:58,757
Perhaps fuel consumption rises without
corresponding increase in output.

93
00:06:59,730 --> 00:07:04,605
Perhaps heat transfer efficiency
gradually deteriorates over months.

94
00:07:05,934 --> 00:07:11,584
These changes may never exceed a
traditional alarm threshold, yet they

95
00:07:11,585 --> 00:07:14,721
still indicate emerging performance loss.

96
00:07:16,138 --> 00:07:21,959
The advantage is seeing the entire
process as a system rather than

97
00:07:22,110 --> 00:07:25,338
evaluating components independently.

98
00:07:26,806 --> 00:07:31,995
Now let's shift from mechanical assets to
one of the most critical electrical assets

99
00:07:31,996 --> 00:07:35,069
in my-- in any plant, like transformer.

100
00:07:36,433 --> 00:07:41,398
Transformers present a unique
reliability challenge because we cannot

101
00:07:41,398 --> 00:07:43,531
directly observe internal conditions.

102
00:07:45,129 --> 00:07:51,644
Most transformer failures begin
internally and progress long

103
00:07:51,655 --> 00:07:53,516
before visible symptoms appear.

104
00:07:53,984 --> 00:07:58,163
That's why dissolved gas
analysis has become so valuable.

105
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:05,320
AI can evaluate historical gas
patterns and compare them with

106
00:08:05,328 --> 00:08:06,957
thousands of previous cases.

107
00:08:08,084 --> 00:08:10,677
Equally important is trend interpretation.

108
00:08:11,634 --> 00:08:14,783
One measurement rarely
tells us the full story.

109
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:21,448
Direction, persistence, and
operating content matter far more.

110
00:08:21,995 --> 00:08:26,730
And because these assets are critical,
explainability becomes essential.

111
00:08:27,495 --> 00:08:30,356
Engineers must understand
why an alert occurred.

112
00:08:31,556 --> 00:08:32,934
Trust requires transparency.

113
00:08:34,626 --> 00:08:36,766
Which leads to another
important challenge here.

114
00:08:37,334 --> 00:08:40,780
How do we present this
information to operators without

115
00:08:40,801 --> 00:08:42,108
overwhelming them, right?

116
00:08:42,129 --> 00:08:45,853
You see, if, if an operator is sitting in
a, in an operating room and you're getting

117
00:08:46,299 --> 00:08:49,268
so many alarms, doesn't make sense, right?

118
00:08:49,291 --> 00:08:50,177
That's not our goal.

119
00:08:51,177 --> 00:08:54,803
In many control rooms, the problem
isn't a lack of information.

120
00:08:55,392 --> 00:08:56,739
It is too much information.

121
00:08:56,762 --> 00:08:59,055
As I was saying, it's
too many alarms, right?

122
00:08:59,162 --> 00:09:00,179
Or too much of data.

123
00:09:00,692 --> 00:09:04,722
So operators frequently
experience alarm flooding.

124
00:09:05,404 --> 00:09:08,655
Hundreds of notifications may
occur during abnormal events.

125
00:09:09,907 --> 00:09:13,346
AI's responsibility is not
to suppress the information.

126
00:09:14,967 --> 00:09:17,541
Its responsibility to
organize the information.

127
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:23,377
Instead of presenting dozens of
disconnected alarms, systems can

128
00:09:23,379 --> 00:09:27,717
identify likely causes, group
related events, and provide context.

129
00:09:29,131 --> 00:09:32,427
I'm stressing on the term context
because that's the most important

130
00:09:32,428 --> 00:09:33,773
thing we've been discussing so far.

131
00:09:35,192 --> 00:09:39,576
This helps operators build
situational awareness more quickly.

132
00:09:40,441 --> 00:09:45,319
Importantly, the decision authority
remains with the human operator.

133
00:09:46,509 --> 00:09:50,244
AI supports the judgment,
but it does not replace it.

134
00:09:51,884 --> 00:09:55,795
Of course, none of this works unless pe-
unless people trust the system, though.

135
00:09:57,716 --> 00:10:00,410
Technology adoption is
ultimately a human challenge.

136
00:10:00,778 --> 00:10:03,883
Users, trust the systems
they can understand.

137
00:10:04,512 --> 00:10:07,518
That's why explainability is critical.

138
00:10:08,451 --> 00:10:11,896
Show the signal, show the
reasoning, show the evidence.

139
00:10:12,399 --> 00:10:15,596
AI must also respect existing workflows.

140
00:10:16,837 --> 00:10:21,047
If it creates an additional
complexity, adoption suffers.

141
00:10:22,258 --> 00:10:27,498
And finally, users must retain the
ability to challenge the recommendations.

142
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:32,749
The best solutions create partnership,
not de- not the dependency.

143
00:10:34,593 --> 00:10:38,641
When trust is built successfully,
organizations begin realizing

144
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:40,633
measurable business value

145
00:10:41,633 --> 00:10:46,540
The value of AI extends
beyond maintenance efficiency.

146
00:10:47,421 --> 00:10:50,517
Earlier warnings provide
more time for investigation.

147
00:10:51,492 --> 00:10:56,465
Condition-based planning reduce-- reduces
unnecessary maintenance activities.

148
00:10:57,273 --> 00:11:01,661
Emerging risks can be identified
before safety consequences

149
00:11:01,686 --> 00:11:07,205
appear, and digital evidence
improves compliance documentation.

150
00:11:08,776 --> 00:11:14,571
The most significant opportunity,
however, emerges at enterprise level.

151
00:11:15,413 --> 00:11:21,410
When data is aggregated across
multiple plants, recurring reliability

152
00:11:21,410 --> 00:11:26,478
patterns become visible, organizations
become learning collectively

153
00:11:26,484 --> 00:11:27,816
rather than asset by asset.

154
00:11:28,816 --> 00:11:34,248
Despite these benefits, deployment
is not without a challenge.

155
00:11:35,248 --> 00:11:40,206
Most AI project-- projects fail
for reasons that have little

156
00:11:40,227 --> 00:11:41,708
to do with the algorithms.

157
00:11:42,602 --> 00:11:47,407
The common challenges are the data
quality, integration complexity,

158
00:11:47,917 --> 00:11:51,247
explainability requirements,
and cybersecurity concerns.

159
00:11:52,042 --> 00:11:57,387
That's why successful deployment begins
with understanding the maintenance

160
00:11:57,401 --> 00:12:00,172
problem, not by selecting a model.

161
00:12:01,109 --> 00:12:03,335
Alignment is equally important.

162
00:12:04,703 --> 00:12:09,350
Reliability engineers, operators,
planners, data specialists,

163
00:12:10,177 --> 00:12:14,059
and cybersecurity teams all
define success differently.

164
00:12:15,099 --> 00:12:19,295
The implementation framework shows
here provides a practical roadmap.

165
00:12:20,385 --> 00:12:21,483
Define the problem.

166
00:12:22,293 --> 00:12:24,593
Build reliable data foundations.

167
00:12:25,368 --> 00:12:27,512
Develop and validate models.

168
00:12:28,357 --> 00:12:31,830
Deploy under governance
and continuous feedback.

169
00:12:32,835 --> 00:12:38,484
Focus on operational outcomes rather
than analytics for analytics' sake.

170
00:12:38,790 --> 00:12:42,075
You see, there's no point in you
showing too many graphs, right?

171
00:12:42,193 --> 00:12:43,628
That's, that's not the goal, right?

172
00:12:44,083 --> 00:12:46,267
It's a-- it's more about
an operational outcome.

173
00:12:47,746 --> 00:12:52,444
Once the organizations successfully
establish these foundations, the next

174
00:12:52,444 --> 00:12:54,518
wave of innovation becomes possible.

175
00:12:56,121 --> 00:12:57,343
Let's talk about future.

176
00:12:58,264 --> 00:13:01,028
The future extends beyond
predictive maintenance.

177
00:13:02,196 --> 00:13:06,620
Digital twins will combine live
operational data with engineering models.

178
00:13:07,789 --> 00:13:12,484
Autonomous robotics will expand
inspection capabilities in

179
00:13:12,626 --> 00:13:14,470
hazardous or difficult areas.

180
00:13:15,686 --> 00:13:21,385
Fleet analytics will allow lessons learned
in one plant to benefit dozens of others.

181
00:13:22,444 --> 00:13:25,637
The future isn't simply smarter
algorithm, it's intelligent

182
00:13:25,656 --> 00:13:31,047
ecosystem that continuously learn
across assets and facilities.

183
00:13:32,038 --> 00:13:37,161
Which brings us to the central message
of today's discussion If there is one

184
00:13:37,175 --> 00:13:44,161
takeaway I hope you all remember, it's
this: AI is not replacing maintenance

185
00:13:44,179 --> 00:13:46,283
engineering, it is strengthening it.

186
00:13:47,136 --> 00:13:52,508
The future of maintenance involves
connecting plant data with engineering

187
00:13:52,509 --> 00:13:57,364
knowledge, anticipating problems
before they become failures, and

188
00:13:57,367 --> 00:14:02,276
sustaining continuous insight rather
than relying on periodic observations.

189
00:14:02,932 --> 00:14:07,916
Organizations that com-- successfully
combine human expertise with the

190
00:14:07,969 --> 00:14:13,821
AI-assisted intelligence will achieve
higher reliability, lower risk,

191
00:14:13,888 --> 00:14:16,233
and more informed decision-making.

192
00:14:18,071 --> 00:14:19,703
Thank you for your time and attention.

193
00:14:20,242 --> 00:14:25,297
As power generation becomes increasingly
data-rich, the organizations that

194
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:30,036
transform information into actionable
maintenance intelligence will define the

195
00:14:30,036 --> 00:14:32,348
next generation of reliability excellence.

196
00:14:33,626 --> 00:14:35,830
Thank you for your time
and thank you, everyone.
